WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.320
Razer was the gaming brand.
Maybe you owned one of these mice or Keyboards.&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.800
Over $1.6 billion in revenue. A&nbsp;
$4.4 billion market cap. A huge IPO.&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.920
Then… they delisted their stock?
What happened?&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.200
As it turns out, Razer’s story is wild.
They didn’t really “crash”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.320
something else happened.
A Razer finance platform,&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.280
A Lamborghini partnership,
An energy drink brand,&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:36.160
And now, AI Holograms.
What happened to Razer?

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:48.720
Razer's current decisions are even more&nbsp;
confusing, when we look back at what they were.&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.160
Razer helped redefine gaming hardware.
It really influenced everything, from RGB lighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.560
to esports gear, but they also did something else.
They pushed the entire industry forward.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.880
In 2005, Razer had just emerged from bankruptcy,&nbsp;
and two people bought the rights to the brand:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.560
Min-Liang Tan and Robert Krakoff.
But, these weren't your typical founders.&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.880
Tan was a lawyer, and Krakoff was&nbsp;
actually a professional NFL running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.320
back, before he moved into a tech job.
And they were eying up a booming industry.&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.640
After a short decline in&nbsp;
2001, PC sales were growing.&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:35.200
3% growth in 2002, then 27% in 2003.
2005 saw almost 240 million sales.&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.800
And inside that market was PC gaming.
Games like World of Warcraft had just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.960
launched, which made $391 million in&nbsp;
2005 from subscriptions and expansions.&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.360
Competitive shooters like Counter-Strike&nbsp;
were taking off, which required precise aim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.000
and mouse and keyboard, not controller.
But, these two just really loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.800
playing video games.
In fact, they just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.520
to do one thing: Make a really good mouse.
[Min-Liang Tan]: "Is there a possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.400
for us to build a better mouse? All we&nbsp;
wanted to do was to be able to compete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.880
with the rest of the gamers out there."
So their slogan, at least in the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.720
was "For gamers. By gamers."
[Min-Liang Tan]: "If you're really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.640
designing a product for yourself,&nbsp;
you'll design the best product."&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.920
This was actually why they&nbsp;
bought Razer in the first place.&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.840
Though they weren't alone. They had just&nbsp;
got some pretty hefty funding from Hong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:34.640
Kong billionaire Li Ka-shing and Temasek Holdings.
So, they got to work, and after a few iterations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.720
they had it:
The DeathAdder.&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.720
Crafted to fit multiple hand grips,&nbsp;
high sensitivity, side buttons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.680
everything was carefully designed for PC&nbsp;
gaming. It even had a left-handed version.&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.480
It looks pretty basic by today's&nbsp;
standards, but this thing took off.&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.320
Professional players and teams&nbsp;
started using it, like Team Liquid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.440
or even League of Legends player Faker who won&nbsp;
three world championships with the DeathAdder.&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:05.200
To date, this mouse has over 20 million sales.
After professionals started using the DeathAdder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.280
and performing very well with it,&nbsp;
millions of others jumped on board.&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.400
Razer was now unstoppable.
So they branched out, but here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.960
Razer did something a bit unexpected.
You might know this sound.&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.720
You might even own one!
These are mechanical keyboards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:32.160
They're snappy, responsive, satisfying to type,&nbsp;
and are huge in gaming, largely because of Razer.&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.160
But they didn't invent these mechanical&nbsp;
switches, the company Cherry did in the 1980s.&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.440
But, many of these keyboards were&nbsp;
industrial, and bulky. Not really "gaming".&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.360
So, in 2010, Razer launched the&nbsp;
Blackwidow, and it took off.&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.400
Some people are still using&nbsp;
these keyboards to this day.&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.480
So Razer kept going.
Gaming laptops in 2013. But not bulky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.160
they were sleek, thin, like a MacBook Pro.
Then in 2018 came another breakthrough:&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.000
The Razer Huntsman, with their&nbsp;
own optical keyboard switches.&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.560
It could survive 100 million keystrokes&nbsp;
versus Cherry MX's 50 million.&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.760
A single key could be pressed 10,000 times&nbsp;
a day, for 27 years, before it broke.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.760
By this point, Razer had built&nbsp;
a genuinely great reputation.&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.640
Its gear was great, it felt&nbsp;
engineered to be great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.360
and their products pushed the industry forward.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.280
But also, the brand had this&nbsp;
cool, rebellious feel to it.&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.120
Every major PC game was lining&nbsp;
up to partner with Razer.&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.800
Their products were selling&nbsp;
millions of units, and by 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.520
they had $390 million in revenue.
So it was time for the next step.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.400
They raised $528 million in their&nbsp;
IPO on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange.&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.240
The stock surged by as much as 42%, it&nbsp;
was one of the biggest IPOs of the year.&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.000
Everything was going great.&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.200
It seemed like the start of a&nbsp;
new chapter… so what went wrong?&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:13.840
Right when the company hit its peak… Razer&nbsp;
started making some very odd choices.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.800
Razer was at the top of their game. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.000
around this time, they began to make&nbsp;
some moves you'd describe as… weird.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.960
In 2020 Razer launched its&nbsp;
first gaming chair. Razer Iskur.&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.640
Then in January 2022 they announced&nbsp;
a modular gaming desk: Project Sophia

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.120
A PC inside the desk, a giant&nbsp;
OLED screen, modular parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.920
full RGB Lighting, all in one.
It never came out.&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.120
The odd part was that they weirdly&nbsp;
focused on it being a work-from-home desk?&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.080
Razer also tried building its own gaming console&nbsp;
after acquiring the software assets from Ouya in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:06:02.720
2015. It was discontinued the following year.
Then in 2018, they bought THX. Yes, that THX.&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.200
This was to use THX's expertise in audio&nbsp;
and visual quality to expand into areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.520
like virtual reality and live streaming.
Then, they entered the smartphone market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.120
by acquiring Nextbit, leading to&nbsp;
the launch of the Razer Phone.&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.520
The first smartphone built for gamers, featuring&nbsp;
a 120Hz refresh rate (wow) and a 5.7-inch display.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.920
It seemed genuinely great for gaming.
But after two versions, Razer laid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.280
off most of its mobile division staff.
Then came RESPAWN. An energy brand for gamers.&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:37.200
To be clear, this was originally an April Fools&nbsp;
gag, but it turned into a real product line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.320
with Razer even partnering with&nbsp;
Mars to produce chewing gum.&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.680
And there's plenty more.
A chair collab between Razer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.880
and Panerai, the Italian luxury watchmaker.
A collab between Razer and Lamborghini for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:54.080
a $1,299 gaming chair. In 2022 you&nbsp;
could buy a pretty good PC for that.&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:00.560
Razer was becoming a premium "lifestyle" brand&nbsp;
instead of a product one. It's definitely a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:06.560
to have higher margins… if you pull it off.
But… it gets worse.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.320
In 2018, Razer made one of&nbsp;
its strangest moves yet.&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:15.200
They launched a fintech company.
Razer Fintech, an e-wallet service targeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:21.040
teenagers and millennials in Malaysia.
But, something unexpected happened.&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.040
It… worked?
It became one of Southeast Asia's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.520
largest offline-to-online payment networks.
By 2023 total payment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.840
volume was over $6.6 billion.
Then Razer decided to make banking… gamer-themed.&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.920
In 2020, they partnered with&nbsp;
Visa to launch the Razer Card.&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.840
A prepaid card with cashback&nbsp;
rewards and gamified feature&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:49.040
The card literally lit up when you paid.
It also shut down a year later.&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:55.600
But surprisingly, the fintech business&nbsp;
survived, and was re-branding into Fiuu in 2024.&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:01.760
By this point, fans of Razer were very confused.
They still made gaming products, but they seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.240
distracted with other things.
Razer kept asking "Can we?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:20.320
but never really stopped to ask "Should we?"
This made what happened next a whole lot worse.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:25.040
There are all kinds of business stories&nbsp;
hiding in plain sight, with a lot to teach us.&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:31.040
We cover these stories every week, so&nbsp;
subscribe! We're almost at a million too!

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.040
With all these weird moves,&nbsp;
Razer wasn't doing too well.&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:41.280
In early 2019, they were already burning cash,&nbsp;
losing around $48 million in just six months.&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.280
Then, a stroke of luck… well… for Razer at least.&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:50.320
COVID-19 hit. With millions of people&nbsp;
stuck indoors gaming, demand surged.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:51.920
Just look at that jump.&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:09:01.040
By 2020, Razer finally turned profitable…&nbsp;
but just $800,000 on $1.2 billion in revenue.&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.080
In 2021, things looked much stronger.
Profit rose to $43.4 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:10.960
while revenue climbed to $1.62 billion&nbsp;
on a 33% year-over-year increase.&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:17.440
Everything seemed to be going great.
But then, they delisted from the stock exchange.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.560
Why go private… were they secretly dying?

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.360
Razer's stock wasn't really actively trading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:26.480
meaning very few investors&nbsp;
were buying or selling it.&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:32.720
Low activity can make a company look smaller than&nbsp;
it actually is, even if revenue is growing a ton.&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.000
Razer's stock price was pretty&nbsp;
much flat the entire time.&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.800
At this point, Razer was valued at around&nbsp;
$4.3 billion, though Razer thought they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.800
were worth much more.
So they pulled the stock.&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:52.080
But things only became more difficult from here.
By 2024, revenue was essentially the same as 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.920
about $708 million, while profit collapsed&nbsp;
from $35.4 million to just $3 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.400
a drop of more than 90%.
Then, Razer pulled its laptops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.840
from US stores, one of its biggest products.
New US tariffs hit key manufacturing regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.040
like China, Taiwan, and Vietnam, disrupting&nbsp;
the supply chain behind its hardware.&nbsp;

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.600
It just wasn't worth the cost.
Then came the memory crisis.&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:25.600
DRAM rose 90–95% and NAND flash&nbsp;
55–60% in early 2026, thanks to AI.&nbsp;

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:31.760
Even companies like HP noted that memory alone&nbsp;
now accounts for nearly 35% of a PC's cost.&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:37.360
It's not the best time to be a premium gamer&nbsp;
brand, or a company selling to those gamers.&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.200
In 2022 Tan said "we saw a deceleration in the&nbsp;
growth momentum for our products and services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:47.760
since the second half of 2021 compared&nbsp;
to exceptional growth in the prior year;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.720
we expect this trend to continue through 2022",
It was only getting worse.&nbsp;

00:10:52.720 --> 00:11:01.680
Razer.com online sales were $297.6M in 2025,&nbsp;
down 5-10% from the previous year. 2026 revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.520
is expected to decline as much as 20-50%.
There were macro factors of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:13.280
component costs, supply chains, tariffs,&nbsp;
but I think there's something bigger.&nbsp;

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:17.920
But before we get to that,
Razer was ready to make one more bold move.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:24.400
In 2026, Razer jumped into the AI trend with&nbsp;
a new idea, a 3D holographic desk companion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:28.800
designed to sit right on your setup.
The project, called PROJECT AVA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:36.080
uses xAI's Grok technology to basically, from&nbsp;
what I can gather… be an AI Esports Coach.&nbsp;

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:40.400
Fans weren't really happy.
"Razer will do anything except make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.960
good companion software for their hardware"
"Focusing on making better products and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:50.240
having decent options for people who don't have&nbsp;
thousands to drop on PC parts/peripherals/software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:56.560
willy-nilly, thereby ensuring a smaller but much&nbsp;
more steady profit stream? Nah, let's get in on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:02.160
that AI grift like everyone else is!"
And, to be honest, they're spot on.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:07.200
Razer, to put it simply, got distracted.
They had one of the most trusted PC gaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:12.240
brands. And one based on quality as well!
But not anymore.&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:15.920
Razer is now basically a "gaming lifestyle brand".&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:21.600
But… many customers don’t care about that.&nbsp;
They miss when Razer just made great products.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:26.400
" I miss the old Razer build quality"
"Am I crazy or did Razer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:31.120
have more "soul" back then?"
"Quality was outstanding back then."&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.800
It's good to have a brand in PC gaming, but&nbsp;
people want reliable, high-performance products.&nbsp;

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:41.760
I used to actually have a Razer keyboard,&nbsp;
and mouse. They were pretty good!&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:48.400
But now, competitors like Logitech, Keychron,&nbsp;
Corsair, and ASUS have swallowed their market.&nbsp;

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:53.760
They basically did the opposite of&nbsp;
Razer: refined, and doubled down.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.800
Razer isn't the only brand&nbsp;
to lose their way though.&nbsp;

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:01.040
Dell was also one of the most reliable&nbsp;
and trusted brands, until they released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:11.440
one of the worst laptops in history.
